Do-It-Together (DiT) maintenance approaches foster collaborative relationships between users and service providers, creating shared responsibility for product care and repair. Unlike traditional repair models where professionals work alone or purely professional maintenance, DiT engages users in the repair process, building practical knowledge and strengthening emotional connections to products. This approach extends beyond individual empowerment to create community learning ecosystems where expertise is shared, skills are developed, and maintenance becomes a social activity rather than a isolated transaction.

Designing for DiT requires creating accessible repair environments, providing clear guidance without requiring advanced expertise, and structuring interactions that welcome diverse skill levels. The shift from 'Do-It-Yourself' to 'Do-It-Together' recognizes that many people benefit from guided support and shared learning spaces. Organizations like Repair Cafés demonstrate how this approach can build community resilience, reduce waste, and create meaningful human connections around product stewardship.